    {
      "id": 119,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        2220,
        -860
      ],
      "size": [
        480,
        560
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "title": "SEMANTIC BRIDGE",
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "SEMANTIC BRIDGE — over the PROMPT SPAN only\n\nWorkflow 22's generation pass with the PDD upscale removed. Every\ngeneration setting is untouched: res_multistep, simple / 20 / 1.0,\nshift 12/3, ref_image_size max, the H3 Character anchors and voice.\n\nWHAT THE NODE DOES\n  A distilled adapter (5120 -> 512 -> 512 -> 5120, SiLU) rewrites the\n  conditioning and is blended back in:\n\n      hybrid = h + alpha * (projected - h)\n\n  It targets what H3 recognises but mis-relates: which hand holds what,\n  left/right ordering, transparent vs reflective, and whether an action\n  was explicitly NOT requested.\n\nWHY `apply_to` IS `prompt only`\n  The published node blends EVERY token, and its author states plainly\n  that ref2va is unsupported and that reference AUDIO degraded lip-sync.\n  The reason is distributional: in ref2va the conditioning reads\n\n      <Picture 1>: [vision] ... <Audio 1>: <the prompt>\n\n  and those reference tokens never existed in the fl2va data the adapter\n  was trained on. The prompt is a contiguous SUFFIX -- the tokenizer emits\n  every reference first and calls add_text(prompt) last -- so masking the\n  trailing tokens leaves every reference block byte-identical.\n\n  `everything` reproduces the published behaviour, for comparison.\n\nTHE PROMPT INPUT IS WIRED, NOT TYPED. It comes from the same H3 Scene\nPrompt output the reference node gets. If it ever differs, the span is\ncomputed from the wrong string and the wrong tokens are transformed.\n\nREAD THE INFO OUTPUT ON THE FIRST RUN. It reports\n\n    prompt span = last N of T tokens (X%); Y reference token(s) untouched\n\nIf it says `not a usable suffix`, the count did not line up and the blend\nfell back to the whole sequence -- the result is then the published\nbehaviour, not ours.\n\nalpha 0.10 is the author's recommendation. 0.15 is what their published\nA/B examples used to make the difference visible. 0 is a passthrough.\n\nUNPROVEN. The adapter's evidence is representation fidelity plus two\nqualitative clips. Nothing measures video quality, and nothing has been\nmeasured on ref2va at all."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
