    {
      "id": 125,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        2660,
        -1560
      ],
      "size": [
        500,
        840
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "title": "KEYFRAME TIMELINE",
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "KEYFRAME TIMELINE — a picture at a time on the clip\n\nRun this AFTER the reference node. It attaches keyframes to the\nCONDITIONING with a timestamp; it does not write into the video you are\nmaking. That distinction is the whole reason it can work at any time at\nall -- see below.\n\nFOUR SLOTS. Each is an image plus a time in SECONDS. -1 disables a slot.\n  time_1 = 0.0    slot 1 at the first frame -- the PROVEN case\n  time_2 = 3.0    mid-clip -- the UNTESTED one\n  slots 3 and 4 disabled\n\nSTART WITH time_2 = -1 and confirm the first-frame case behaves. Then set\nit to 3.0 and see whether the shot actually arrives at that image at\nthree seconds.\n\nLENGTH IS 141 AND MUST MATCH the reference node. 141 is a legal run\n(17*8+5) and divides by 3, so it sits on the video and audio clocks both.\nThe node snaps internally, but a mismatch means the keyframe lands at the\nwrong time.\n\nWHY THIS IS NOT \"STICKING A FRAME IN THE LATENT\"\n\n  The video is stored in GROUPS, sized 1, 4, 4, 4, 4 repeating. Most\n  groups hold four frames mashed together; every fifth holds one. So the\n  single-frame slots are frames 0, 17, 34, 51 and so on -- the first\n  frame is not the only one.\n\n  But the compressor works FORWARDS: when it builds the slot for frame 17\n  it is also carrying memory of the sixteen frames before it. Drop a\n  standalone photo in there and you have written something that could\n  never have come out of a real video. Wave 8a measured that: error\n  roughly DOUBLED per link, and the model paid for it in contrast.\n\n  Frame 0 is the only position with no history, so a standalone photo is\n  exactly right there and nowhere else.\n\n  A KEYFRAME SIDESTEPS ALL OF IT. It is a conditioning row carrying a\n  time coordinate, sitting outside the target video in the packed\n  sequence. It never has to be a valid latent at that position. That is\n  why it works anywhere, and it is the same mechanism core's\n  MiniMaxH3AddGuide uses -- ours takes four in one node and coexists with\n  reference images, which stock could not do until the layout was\n  patched.\n\nFIRST REAL EXERCISE OF THE ROW-ORDER FIX. Until 2026-08-31 the patched\nlayout emitted reference rows before keyframe rows while core built the\nlatent list keyframes-first, so every reference latent landed in a\nkeyframe's slot whenever a graph used both. Nothing errored; the totals\nmatched and the contents shifted. This graph uses both, so if identity\ngoes strange with a keyframe attached, that is the first place to look."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
